INT. HARTWIG OBSERVATORY - DOME CHAMBER - NIGHT

A great brass telescope tilts toward a slit of stars. Star charts blanket every wall. A desk lamp, a cold teapot, a black intercom box on the wall.

PROFESSOR EMIL HARTWIG, 60s, rumpled tweed, ink on his cuffs, sits hunched over a ledger. He looks up at the intercom as it crackles.

CORA (V.O.)
(through the intercom)
Professor? Are you still up there?

HARTWIG
Who is that?

CORA (V.O.)
The night porter's niece. They let me in at the gate. I need to ask you about the Calloway disappearance.

Hartwig closes the ledger very carefully.

HARTWIG
I don't discuss that. Good night.

CORA (V.O.)
Your assistant was last seen climbing these stairs. Eleven years ago tonight.

HARTWIG
Is that what the papers say?

CORA (V.O.)
That is what the logbook says. The one in your hand.

Hartwig's fingers tighten on the ledger. He glances at the dome door.

HARTWIG
The door at the bottom is locked.

CORA (V.O.)
I know. I'm calling from the landing.

A knock, three soft raps, echoes up the iron staircase. Hartwig stands. His chair scrapes.

HARTWIG
How did you get a key?

CORA (V.O.)
I didn't. It was never locked from my side.

The dome door swings open. CORA BELLINGHAM, 30s, rain-soaked trench coat, a brass key on a chain around her neck, steps into the lamplight. She holds a small handset against her ear and clicks it off.

CORA
Hello, Professor.

HARTWIG
You're not the porter's niece.

CORA
No.

HARTWIG
Then who?

CORA
Daniel Calloway was my brother.

Hartwig takes a step back and bumps the telescope. It swings an inch and groans.

HARTWIG
I was cleared. There was an inquiry.

CORA
There was. It asked the wrong questions.

She walks to the desk and puts the brass key on the ledger.

CORA (CONT'D)
That's the key to the sub-basement. Daniel's key. It was in his coat, in the river.

HARTWIG
That proves nothing.

CORA
It proves he had a reason to go down there. Why would an astronomer go down?

HARTWIG
Cora--

CORA
You said my name. I didn't give it.

Hartwig stops. The lamp hums. A moth circles it.

HARTWIG
Heard it from the porter. At the gate.

CORA
The porter retired in March.

Silence. Hartwig sits slowly in his chair.

HARTWIG
He fell, you understand. There was an instrument case on the stair. A terrible accident.

CORA
Then why hide it?

HARTWIG
Because he wasn't alone on the stair.

He opens the ledger and turns it toward her. A page of neat handwriting, and below it a second hand, shakier.

HARTWIG (CONT'D)
Read the last line. Not mine.

Cora leans over the page. Her face does not change, but her hand finds the edge of the desk.

CORA
(reading, quietly)
"I pushed him. Forgive me. - T. Wexler."

HARTWIG
The old director. He died that winter. I've kept this page for eleven years, because I didn't know who to hand it to.

CORA
You could have handed it to the police.

HARTWIG
And be asked why I waited?

CORA
(beat)
Yes. Why did you wait?

Hartwig looks up at the slit of sky, where a single bright star hangs.

HARTWIG
Because I was afraid he would come back and ask me the same thing.
